/**

@page page_tutorials_exahype2_numerics_higher_order_finite_volumes Higher Order Finite Volumes

\tableofcontents

With standard finite volume methods we assume that the solution is constant within each volume.
We can construct higher order schemes in space by relaxing this assumption.

# MUSCL Schemes
MUSCL (Monotonic Upstream-centred Scheme for Conservation Laws) schemes assume that the solution is a piecewise linear function
@f$
    u^n(x) = u_i^n + (x-x_i)\frac{s_i}{\Delta x}, x_{i-\frac{1}{2}} < x < x_{i+\frac{1}{2}}
@f$
where @f$ s_i = ave(u^n_{i+1} - u^n_i, u^n_i - u^n_{i-1}) @f$ is the slope at volume @f$ i @f$.

The left and right face values are calculated as
@f{eqnarray*}
    u^n_{i-\frac{1}{2}} = u^i_n - \frac{1}{2}s_i
    u^n_{i+\frac{1}{2}} = u^i_n + \frac{1}{2}s_i
@f}

The update of @f$ u_i^n @f$ with the flux contributions reads these face values.
@f$
    u^{n + 1}_i = u^n_i + \frac{\Delta t}{\Delta x}(F_{i-\frac{1}{2}} - F_{i+\frac{1}{2}})
@f$
@f$ F_{i-\frac{1}{2}} and F_{i+\frac{1}{2}} @f$ are the solutions to the Riemann problem at each surrounding face.
There are many approaches to solve the Riemann problem, but we use the Rusanov flux.

# The MUSCL-Hancock Scheme
The MUSCL-Hancock scheme modifies the standard MUSCL approach. It computes the flux half a time step later.
The flux and the source term then update the face values.

*/
